// Bitonic sort — power-of-2 length compare-exchange network.
//
// Reads a length-N i32 input, returns the sorted permutation. The
// bitonic-sort algorithm is a STATIC compare-exchange network: which
// element pairs compare-exchange at each stage depends only on the
// stage index, not on the values. The network has log2(N) outer
// stages, each with k inner sub-stages, where the k-th outer stage
// runs k inner stages (total log2(N) * (log2(N)+1) / 2 = 21 inner
// stages for N=64).
//
// PRD §9 row 12: "Static communication pattern, fits SDF." Despite
// being a SORT (each comparator's output depends on its input
// values), the COMMUNICATION pattern is fully scheduled at compile
// time — a clean fit for the synchronous-dataflow model.
//
// What this example WAS originally meant to demonstrate (per cycle-171
// brief): a multi-stage compare-exchange network whose parallel
// schedule places N/2 compare-exchange comparators on distinct
// workers per stage, with a barrier between stages. The headline
// claim is "static communication pattern fits SDF" — the schedule
// can pre-allocate the per-stage worker assignments because they
// are known at compile time from N alone.
//
// Why ONE big kernel and not stage-decomposed at the algorithm level
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Same fundamental v2 sublanguage limits hit by 04-prefix-sum
// (TASK-0179) and 10-wavefront (TASK-0044.05.01):
//   1. Each stage k operates on a DIFFERENT pairing of elements
//      (the bit-pattern of k determines which pairs swap). Encoding
//      this at the algorithm level requires either (a) log2(N) *
//      (log2(N)+1) / 2 separate `for` blocks with hardcoded stage
//      bit-patterns (21 blocks for N=64 — feasible but tedious and
//      brittle), or (b) loop bounds dependent on a loop variable
//      (`for k : 0..STAGES { for pair : 0..pair_count(k) { ... } }`)
//      — rejected by the const-loop-bound rule (the ACFG builder
//      only `eval_const`s bounds).
//   2. The compare-exchange writes BOTH elements of the pair
//      (output[a] = min, output[b] = max). v2's single-assignment-
//      per-data-symbol rule treats `output` as one symbol; the
//      kernel-level masking trick (see 04-prefix-sum's
//      `block_scan`) works for accumulator-shaped reductions but
//      NOT for permutations.
//
// The honest v2 encoding is therefore: ONE kernel call that takes
// `input` and returns the whole sorted `output`. The stage-by-stage
// compare-exchange network lives in `kernels::bitonic_sort` (real
// Rust). The algorithm declares only the dataflow:
//
//   input  <-- load_input();
//   output <-- bitonic_sort(input);
//   save_output(output);
//
// The cost: there is no algorithm-level decomposition handle the
// schedule sublanguage can use to express a stage-parallel schedule.
// The stage-parallel schedule is GRAMMAR-DEFERRED (filed as a
// follow-up); v2 only ships the naive schedule.
//
// Numeric / value choice: i32 input values, distinct (no equal-key
// stability question to worry about). PRD §10.1 — integer arithmetic
// is bit-deterministic across schedules and backends; comparison
// (min/max) is bit-deterministic by language definition. The
// committed fixture (see README) is a permutation of [0, N) plus a
// constant offset, so sorted output is the corresponding increasing
// sequence; sort correctness is checkable by inspection.

const N : usize = 64;       // power of 2; 64 * 4 = 256 bytes input

// Whole-program input/output. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1.
data input  : i32[N];
data output : i32[N];

// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated.
// Aggregate types; on the Rust side they use `Vec<i32>` (TASK-0103
// canonical convention).
kernel load_input  : ()       -> i32[N]   effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[N]) -> ()       effectful;

// Pure compute kernel. Takes the input array, returns the sorted
// array. The compare-exchange network lives entirely in kernel Rust;
// the algorithm sees only the dataflow edge `input -> bitonic_sort ->
// output`. Aggregate-typed both sides (TASK-0012 TypeMismatch surface,
// identical to examples 01..07/10).
kernel bitonic_sort : (i32[N])  -> i32[N]   pure;

// Dataflow: load, sort, save. Three statements; no loops. The
// algorithm/schedule split here reduces to "which worker runs the
// load / sort / save" — no decomposition axis is exposed at the
// algorithm level (would require grammar extensions for stage-by-
// stage parallelism; see header).
input  <-- load_input();
output <-- bitonic_sort(input);
save_output(output);
